L000587	We can obtain an explicit expression of the integral $I(N,R)$ using the identities given by Eqs.\eqref{eq:gammaidentities};
L000588	we obtain,
L000589	\[
L000590	I(N,R) = \sum_{k=0}^N \abs{c_k}^2\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k}
L000591	\left[1 -\left(1+R^2+\frac{R^4}{2!} +  \cdots +  \frac{R^{2k}}{k!} \right) e^{-R^2} \right]
L000592	\]
L000593	where as before (see Eq.~\eqref{eq:binomargt}) $\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k}$ can be written as
L000594	\[
L000595	
L000596	\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k} = \left(1-\frac{1}{N}\right)\left(1-\frac{2}{N}\right)\left(1-\frac{3}{N}\right)\cdots \left(1-\frac{k-1}{N}\right) <1.
L000597	\]
L000598	
L000599	Now we can make assumptions on the sequence $c_k$ an see explicitly  their effect in the integral. Suppose that for an arbitrary small positive $\epsilon$ we can find two integers  $K_0$ and $N$ such that for all $K\ge K_0$ we have
L000600	$\sum_{k=K+1}^N \abs{c_k}^2\leq \epsilon$.
L000601	Then the sum representing the integral can be divided in 2 terms:
L000602	\[
L000603	I(N,R) = S(K,N,R) + \overline{S}(K,N,R)
L000604	\]
L000605	where
L000606	\[
L000607	S(K,N,R) = \sum_{k=0}^K \abs{c_k}^2\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k} \left[1 -\left(1+R^2+\frac{R^4}{2!} +  \cdots +  \frac{R^{2k}}{k!} \right) e^{-R^2} \right]
L000608	\]
L000609	and
L000610	\[
L000611	\overline{S}(K,N,R) =  \sum_{k=K+1}^N \abs{c_k}^2\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k} \left[1 -\left(1+R^2+\frac{R^4}{2!} +  \cdots +  \frac{R^{2k}}{k!} \right) e^{-R^2} \right]
L000612	\]
L000613	It it is clear that $\overline{S}(K,N,R)  \leq \epsilon$.
L000614	Indeed the factor in the square bracket is less that 1 and the factor $\binom{N}{k}\frac{k!}{N^k}$ is also less than 1 because of Eq.~\eqref{eq:binomargt};
